u=\\frac{R\\sin^{2}\\theta}{r},  where R is the radius of the NS surface, and u is a dimensionless constant. The curvature radius of the field line is (for \\theta\\lesssim 0.5)

\\rho=\\frac{r(1+3\\cos^{2}\\theta)^{3/2}}{3\\sin\\theta(1+\\cos^{2}\\theta)}\\approx \\frac{4r}{3\\sin\\theta}. 

For \\gamma_{e}=300, the curvature radius is estimated to be \\rho\\simeq 1.9\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{cm} to produce \\sim GHz curvature radiation.

